  "regulatory_constraints": [
    {
      "type": "Technical",
      "requirement": "Inverter's limited short-circuit current (in general, 1.1 p.u.–1.5 p.u. compared to 6 p.u.–8 p.u. of synchronous machines) should be given careful attention because it affects system behavior under transformer energization or motor startup, which causes a heavy inrush current, potentially exceeding the inverter rating.",
      "scope": "Offshore wind power plants with inverter-based generation",
      "numerical_value": "1.1 - 1.5",
      "unit": "p.u.",
      "source": "[62]",
      "related_domains": "Technical, Safety"
    },
    {
      "type": "Technical",
      "requirement": "Soft-start techniques, such as inverter voltage ramp-up, can suppress the inrush current during the initial energization, though the black-starting inverters could encounter a significant inrush as they sequentially energize segments of a grid, such as an HVAC step-up transformer, until they obtain enough total generation capacity.",
      "scope": "Offshore wind power plants with inverter-based generation",
      "numerical_value": null,
      "unit": null,
      "source": "[63]",
      "related_domains": "Technical, Safety"
    },
    {
      "type": "Technical",
      "requirement": "In a multiple inverter-driven black start, e.g., an offshore wind power plant where a large number of wind turbines are involved in the task, parallel inverter operation is critical. Leader-follower coordination requiring reliable communications can degrade the robustness of the black start and/or might not be economic or feasible in case the generators are distant, such as in the case of OWPPs, so reliance on communications should be avoided or minimized.",
      "scope": "Offshore wind power plants with multiple inverter-based generators",
      "numerical_value": null,
